From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] luma: Grid paints its own wallpaper, drags, reveals the selection and travels steadily

Grid showed large duplicated, flashing windows behind its cards on a
desktop of libadwaita apps (white rectangles in screenshots). Its wallpaper
was a clone of the desktop background group, which lives inside the window
group. Mutter culls that group every frame against the opaque regions of the
windows above it, whether or not the window group is hidden, so the clone
skipped every rectangle an opaque window covered and nothing painted there:
the display showed stale frames. Grid now builds its own background actor
per display from the same cached backgrounds.

Dragging the row did nothing: the pan sat on the row, which is not reactive,
so it never saw a press. It now belongs to the whole popup, so a drag anywhere
scrolls and never counts as a click.

A long row scrolled from centring its first card to centring its last,
which on a 5120px display opened with half the screen empty and most cards
out of sight. The row now scrolls between its first card on the left margin
and its last on the right margin, opens centred on the window you left where
that range allows, keeps its place when a window changes size, and glides a
card chosen from the keyboard into view.

The travel clock starts after the first frame, which renders every preview
and used to swallow most of the ease-out curve so cards jumped on their first
step. Cards resting wholly outside the row are not painted (they were
rendered at full resolution every frame, at 2x over a neighbouring 1.25x
panel). Size notifications from window previews rebuild the row before the
next frame instead of inside Clutter's layout pass, the close button is shown
by opacity so a hover after layout cannot leave it unallocated, and a Grid
being destroyed is no longer allocated, which logged dozens of
"GridCard has been already disposed" criticals on every dismiss.

Generated against the Shell .83 tree (0122 applied) for release .84.
